WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:06.160
Successful opinion magazines are often launched by successful people.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.460
The New Republic was co-founded

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.140
by the famous progressive intellectual Walter Lippman,

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.950
National Review was launched by the bestselling author William F. Buckley.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.460
Reason on the other hand was the brain child

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.020
of a 20 year old Boston University student

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.130
who wrote in one of his early editors notes,

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.780
I drive a delivery van for a living.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.440
Lanny Friedlander stapled together

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.640
and mailed out the first mimeographed issues

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.410
of Reason from a messy room in his mother's house,

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.020
in Brighton, Massachusetts.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.490
He would sell the fledgling magazine just two years later

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:36.040
and slowly fade away.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.740
And yet Lanny's blazing

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.870
and influential vision still lives on.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.830
Visible in Reason's lowercase, sans serif logo

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.360
it's sympathy for outsiders and dreamers.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.260
And in its hunch that technology, more than politics,

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.410
is the driving force of human freedom.

00:01:05.002 --> 00:01:06.412
Friedlander believed in gathering

00:01:06.412 --> 00:01:08.043
various strains of libertarianism

00:01:08.043 --> 00:01:10.297
under one roof for stimulating debate,

00:01:10.297 --> 00:01:12.422
and was committed to engaging with the best ideas

00:01:12.422 --> 00:01:14.688
of his political adversaries.

00:01:14.688 --> 00:01:17.620
We would just discuss the structure of society

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.360
and what we can do on any areas that we agree upon.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.870
Lanny's mix of startling, visual presentation

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:27.630
and contrarian writing created an almost instant momentum,

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.230
tantalizing early readers and contributors alike

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:33.580
with an infectious sense of unimagined possibilities.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.920
When Reason speaks of poverty, racism, the draft, the war,

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.160
student power, politics, and other vital issues,

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.760
it shall be reasons, not slogans, it gives for conclusions.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.190
Proof, not belligerent assertion. Logic, not legends.

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:52.230
Coherence, not contradictions. This is our promise,

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.840
this is the reason for Reason.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.790
So who was this disheveled visionary

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.410
and why did he flame out so fast?

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:02.060
Upon Freidlander's death in 2011, Reason's Nick Gillespie

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.620
wrote that he had started thinking of Lanny

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.240
as libertarianism's answer to Syd Barrett,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.580
the mad genius founder of Pink Floyd

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.080
who got something great started

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.150
and then couldn't, or wouldn't live in the world

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.710
he did so much to create.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.930
In reality, Lanny Friedlander was an objectivist

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.870
who believed in big tent libertarianism,

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:21.060
a student protestor, reviled other student protestors,

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.090
and anti-war anti draft activists

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.840
who volunteered for the Navy.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.770
He was professionally charismatic and personally introverted

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:31.160
an exacting truth seeker, and unreliable narrator,

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.230
a systemic thinker and disorganized coordinator.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.217
The printed format of this issue, he wrote,

00:02:36.217 --> 00:02:38.590
does not represent a guarantee that the next issue

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.010
will also be printed.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.140
Wired co-creator Louis Rossetto, who first encountered Reason

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.260
as an undergrad at Columbia University, said in 2011

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.840
that the publication was my gateway to good design.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.240
Yet when describing his own self,

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.070
Friedlander preferred the term writer/intellectual.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.690
That writing contained a breadth rarely seen

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.660
among 20-somethings of any era.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.860
Friedlander argued persuasively that pollution is theft

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.060
and applied to Austrian economics to the question

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.890
of how the police would operate in a free society.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:10.160
At turns theoretical and practical, pedantic and poetic,

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.210
his voice vibrated with the visceral rage

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.220
when touching on the dehumanization of modern institutions.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:16.830
Whatever reason the parents might have

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.910
for leaving his children in the care of the state,

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.090
he snarled in a piece about public schools,

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:23.230
it will not diminish their intellectual death

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.350
nor mitigate their emotional torture.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.610
As fate would cruelly have it Friedlander spent much

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.700
of the rest of his life institutionalized

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.770
and chained to the dulling anchor of anti-psychotic drugs.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.050
A diagnosed schizophrenic under the wary eye

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:36.740
of the paternalistic state.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:39.460
But before his sad decline, there was a bracing boldness

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:40.590
to venture somewhere new

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.040
starting with how Lanny's magazine would look.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.610
Visual elements that decades later would become faddish

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:48.370
in art schools were there from the very beginning.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.220
Lanny was a fan of Massimo Vignelli.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:53.160
So Reason had a perfect Swiss grid, very clean and pure

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.620
lots of white space, ragged, right, Helvetica type,

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.690
conceptual illustrations, Rosetto recalled.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:00.870
Friedlander consumed the latest cutting edge books

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:04.040
and periodical graphic design from nearby MIT press

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.150
including mail order newsletters and Zines,

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.830
and of course, comic books.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.030
Reason as early as 1969 published some original panels

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:14.350
by legendary Spider-Man co-creator Steve Ditko.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.530
Reason was anti-establishment without being part

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:18.390
of the new establishment and without being

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:21.600
either crank left or kook right, Rosetto recalled.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.470
And it had this bright, shiny optimism

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:26.490
and unshakable faith in the triumph of good ideas.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.410
In 1968, Friedlander's writing confronted the burning issues

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.440
of the moment, violence, war, race relations,

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.870
campus upheaval, with boldly counter-intuitive takes.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.470
It is the initiation or threat

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.140
of initiation of physical force by the government,

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.307
that is the major cause of violence in America, he wrote.

00:04:43.307 --> 00:04:45.040
Both Jim Crow and minimum wage laws

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.980
were unambiguous examples of racism and the New Left

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.870
and George Wallace had more in common

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:51.390
than either would ever admit.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.600
Lanny was an odd duck, but always striking.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.040
He was a person of few words and conversation

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.770
and always seemed to be somewhat detached

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:58.890
or inward in his way, 

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.780
recalls Mark Frasier, who has a high school student helped

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:04.640
to paste up and other tasks at the magazine.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:06.320
While the libertarian movement was blooming

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.550
in New York City in the early 1970s,

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.260
Lanny was starting to wither declining even to participate

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.430
much when his protege, Louis Rossetto,

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:15.920
invited him to come around the offices of Outlook,

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.320
another influential, if short-lived libertarian publication.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.990
After that, there's a period where he just disappeared.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:23.950
The wheels started coming off more often,

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:25.980
the mental illness that had plagued his mother

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.680
appeared to be derailing a promising young career.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:30.560
Friedlander's final byline in the magazine

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.320
he created came in October, 1972,

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.030
just shy of his 25th birthday.

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:38.300
What came next were vague tales of troubled travels

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.610
in the South, frantic calls for money, a drunk and disorderly

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.280
misdemeanor conviction in Miami in 1975.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.320
Somewhere within that flailing period, Friedlander enlisted

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:50.270
in the Navy only to be discharged shortly thereafter

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:51.940
on psychiatric grounds.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.560
So complete was Friedlander his break

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:56.050
from his Reason-founding past that even his own

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:58.890
attorney didn't believe his friend's old war stories.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:01.530
Lanny was telling me that he was this great graphic artist

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.120
and he started a magazine and everything.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.040
And of course I'm thinking this was just the psychosis, I was all incredulous.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:09.230
Reinforcing that skepticism was the one time

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.950
that Lanny got anxious about losing his art portfolio.

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:14.570
After a search, the missing satchel was discovered

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:16.050
and just like in a beautiful mind,

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.030
the art was like third grade Murphy says,

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:19.680
it was basically jibberish.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.780
Back in 2008, when I was editing Reason,

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.430
I asked my wife who was a private investigator

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:26.050
to help track Friedlander down.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:27.530
Turns out he was in a veteran's home

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:28.900
in Lowell, Massachusetts.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:30.950
She inadvertently got him on the phone.

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:31.783
Is that you?

00:06:31.783 --> 00:06:33.710
She said, yes he responded.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:35.270
She told him that reason wanted to connect

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:37.470
and asked if she could pass along his number.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.610
After a long pause he declined,

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.810
sounding shell-shocked and wounded though not quite mad.

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:44.060
We arranged to have the magazine

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:46.800
and related swag sent to him on a regular basis.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.710
Then in December, 2010, Reason science correspondent

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:51.970
Ronald Bailey got a handwritten letter in the mail

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:54.427
in response to an article about genomics.

00:06:54.427 --> 00:06:56.870
I think you should take your thinking one step further

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:59.210
and write about the prospects for immortality

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:02.627
in the foreseeable future, wrote a Mr. Lanny Friedlander.

00:07:02.627 --> 00:07:04.200
I also wonder if magicians

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.900
can reverse the effects of old age.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.900
Then came the kicker, P.S. I started Reason magazine in 1968.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:13.350
Lanny died of a heart attack

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:16.020
the following year at the age of 63.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:18.500
Lanny Friedlander did more than just start Reason.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.040
He willed into being a demonstration project

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.590
that you don't need money and other built-in advantages

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.110
to launch a worthwhile journal of opinion.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.880
He showed people who didn't even know it yet

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.650
that they were entrepreneurs, journalists, futurists,

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:33.200
and even libertarians, that there was a different way

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.010
of thinking and being in the world.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:36.780
That one could be revolutionary even

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:39.770
while advocating the most modest of policy gains.

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:41.750
Before his productive brain betrayed him,

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:44.100
he was consumed with a question that challenges all

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:45.320
of us to this day.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.220
How do you make social change

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.510
in a world where there is no single right answer?

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:52.670
How do we explore, maximize, and expand all the avenues

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:54.940
for making the world a better place?

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.833
Not that theory isn't important, but it seems to

00:07:57.833 --> 00:08:02.270
be a movement primarily of philosopher kings.

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.240
One person defined the libertarian movement

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.350
as 800 people trading books.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:07.760
We ought to change that.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:10.170
One man can stop the motor of the world,

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:11.880
Ayn Rand wrote in Atlas Shrugged.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:13.820
The opposite is also true.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.310
One man can start the motor,

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.520
not just of one world, but of several.

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.770
That in the end is the legacy of Lanny Friedlander.

